WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:25.769
A RAY OF HOPE:
I’m sure you’ve all heard that Steve Jobs

00:00:25.769 --> 00:00:30.160
died of pancreatic cancer, and technically,
this is true.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:34.640
He died of respiratory arrest due to a pancreatic
neuroendocrine tumor.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:40.640
But, maybe, he didn’t have, or atleast he
could’ve delayed his death by years if not

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:41.700
even a decade.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:47.200
You see, while Steve Jobs did have pancreatic
cancer, there was a silver lining in that

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:51.660
he had a special form of pancreatic cancer
caused by islet cells.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:57.680
Regular pancreatic cancer only has a 5-year
survival rate of 1.8%, but islet cell tumors

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:01.180
have a 5-year survival rate of up to 93%.

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:05.310
Something that swung the odds even more in
Jobs favor was that he found the cancer extremely

00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:06.460
early.

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:10.330
Jobs wasn’t even at the stage at which he
was experiencing symptoms due to the cancer.

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:16.470
In 2003, he was actually suffering from kidney
stones, and when doctors performed a CT scan,

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:19.240
they just happened to notice a shadow on his
pancreas.

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:24.820
So, if Jobs took quick action, he could have
very well extended his lifespan by a substantial

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:27.560
amount, but he chose not to.

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:34.320
In fact, he consistently delayed medical treatment
over and over again for the rest of his life.

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:38.770
For example, given Steve Jobs wealth and status,
he probably could’ve scheduled a surgery

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:41.030
within a week of finding the tumor.

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:45.649
But instead, he decided to wait 9 months by
which point the cancer had already spread

00:01:45.649 --> 00:01:46.840
to his liver.

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:50.740
Another example of Jobs’ stubbornness was
when he refused a liver transplant from Tim

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:53.410
Cook despite desperately needing one.

00:01:53.410 --> 00:01:57.772
Jobs said that he didn’t want his body to
be opened and that he didn’t want to be

00:01:57.772 --> 00:01:59.470
violated in that way.

00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:05.070
And while that’s understandable, this was
literally a choice between life an death.

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:10.050
Also, I don’t think anyone wants to be violated
in that way.

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:14.890
People go through these procedures because
they have to.

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:19.460
But instead, Jobs felt that the best course
of action was to stay on a strict vegan diet

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:22.390
and use herbal remedies and acupuncture.

00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:26.890
While these choices may offer benefits in
day to day life, there’s virtually no scientific

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:29.410
evidence that they help with cancer.

00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:34.140
And given that Jobs was the founder and CEO
of what would eventually become the largest

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:39.000
company in the world, you would think that
he was extremely rational and objective with

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:40.000
these choices.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:43.260
But, that was far from being the case.

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:47.370
People that knew him speculate that he felt
that the problem would just disappear if he

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:48.489
ignored it.

00:02:48.489 --> 00:02:51.950
This line of reasoning had worked for him
in business in the past.

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:56.480
After all, his job was to literally create
unimaginable products and convince the world

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:57.480
to use them.

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:01.620
But, this approach didn’t quite work within
the medical industry.

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:05.940
Aside from Jobs reality distoration glasses,
something that was pushing Jobs to make these

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:10.830
choices was his spiritual life and especially
his beliefs regarding Buddhism and the afterlife.

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:16.269
Jobs even said “Maybe it’s ’cause I
want to believe in an afterlife.

00:03:16.269 --> 00:03:19.500
That when you die, it doesn’t just all disappear.

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:21.299
The wisdom you’ve accumulated.

00:03:21.299 --> 00:03:23.019
Somehow it lives on.”

00:03:23.019 --> 00:03:27.650
So, here’s the story of how Jobs developed
these spiritual beliefs and how they eventually

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:30.530
played a role in his demise.

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:35.890
THE HIPPIE:
Taking a look back, Jobs spiritual beliefs

00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:37.730
predate his cancer by decades.

00:03:37.730 --> 00:03:43.120
In fact, he was a hippie for basically his
entire life which is not that surprising given

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:44.650
the time period in which he grew up.

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:49.129
If you’re not familiar with hippies, these
were a group of people that rejected traditional

00:03:49.129 --> 00:03:53.930
middle class life which they felt was dominated
by materialism and repression.

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:58.050
Instead they embraced openness, peace, and
experimentation.

00:03:58.050 --> 00:04:02.939
They were known for vegetarian diets, holistic
medicine, and of course psychedelics.

00:04:02.939 --> 00:04:07.120
Jobs was especially interested in the latter
and regularly experimented with them throughout

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:08.360
his late teenage years.

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:12.970
For him, this wasn’t just a matter of getting
“high” or trying to live life more fully,

00:04:12.970 --> 00:04:17.280
but it was actually a coping mechanism that
he used to deal with the pain that his birth

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:18.699
parents abandoned him.

00:04:18.699 --> 00:04:23.660
According to Jobs, psychedelics along with
primal screaming opened up stored trauma in

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:25.290
the medulla.

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:28.810
Jobs took many of these habits and beliefs
with him to Apple.

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:33.900
One frowned upon habit that he took with him
was his tendency to not take showers.

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:37.491
Jobs believed that his commitment to a vegan
diet meant that he had flushed out all of

00:04:37.491 --> 00:04:39.139
the mucus from his body.

00:04:39.139 --> 00:04:44.260
So, theoretically, he believed that he shouldn’t
have a body odor and that he didn’t need

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:45.260
showers.

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:47.400
Ironically, this is not even true.

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:51.750
Vegan diets tend to skew away from proteins
which play a significant role in the body’s

00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:53.050
detoxification process.

00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:58.500
So, Jobs’ vegan diet likely meant that he
should’ve taken more showers, not less.

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:01.880
And let’s just say his coworkers strongly
agreed.

00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:05.680
Another unique trait of Jobs was his tendency
to walk around barefoot.

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:10.479
He felt that walking around helped him think
and be more creative, and there is some truth

00:05:10.479 --> 00:05:11.479
to this.

00:05:11.479 --> 00:05:15.840
Moving around does energize our brains and
it reduces stress making it easier to focus

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:17.730
on singular thoughts and ideas.

00:05:17.730 --> 00:05:23.890
But, Jobs didn’t just walk barefoot, he
did some weird things barefoot.

00:05:23.890 --> 00:05:28.720
Jobs liked to sink his feet into cold water
to destress, and this seems completely harmless

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:32.660
at first, and it should be if done like a
normal person.

00:05:32.660 --> 00:05:36.729
Maybe you could fill a bowl up with water
or maybe even buy a pedicure bowl.

00:05:36.729 --> 00:05:41.490
But instead, Jobs sunk his feet into toilets
at the office.

00:05:41.490 --> 00:05:45.740
I suspect that this only made his body odor
worse.

00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:49.660
It wasn’t just his body that he did these
weird experiments on either.

00:05:49.660 --> 00:05:54.310
Jobs also practiced his hippie anti establishment
mindset in other facets of his life as well

00:05:54.310 --> 00:05:55.440
like his car.

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:59.360
Jobs wasn’t a fan of having a license plate
on his car for some reason.

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:03.830
When questioned about it, he simply said that
he didn’t have a licence plate because he

00:06:03.830 --> 00:06:04.940
didn’t have a license plate.

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:09.360
The way that he made this work was by getting
new leases every six months.

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:13.470
California law allowed people to not have
a license plate for the first six months after

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:18.210
getting a new car, and Jobs exploited this
loophole until he died.

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:21.789
It wasn’t till 2019 that California finally
patched this loophole.

00:06:21.789 --> 00:06:27.460
While all of these likely just seem like odd
quirks, Jobs strongly believed in each and

00:06:27.460 --> 00:06:29.259
every one one of them.

00:06:29.259 --> 00:06:33.610
And these unorthodox choices carried over
to his medical choices as well.

00:06:33.610 --> 00:06:39.389
THE BUDDHIST:
If you thought Jobs’ hippie behavior itself

00:06:39.389 --> 00:06:43.530
was extreme, then you haven’t heard about
his spiritual life.

00:06:43.530 --> 00:06:47.950
From his college days, Jobs was especially
interested in Buddhism.

00:06:47.950 --> 00:06:52.080
This interest was initially sparked out of
necessity as opposed to curiosity.

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:56.479
You see, as a broke college student, free
food was a gift.

00:06:56.479 --> 00:07:01.919
And, one of the places that offered free food
was the Hare Krishna temple near Reed college.

00:07:01.919 --> 00:07:06.130
Every Sunday, Jobs walked seven miles to the
temple just to get free food.

00:07:06.130 --> 00:07:10.790
But, very soon, he walked away with much more
than just food.

00:07:10.790 --> 00:07:15.510
Jobs became especially interested in Mahatma
Gandhi and a Hindu sage named Neem Karoli

00:07:15.510 --> 00:07:16.590
Baba.

00:07:16.590 --> 00:07:20.520
Pretty soon, Jobs was talking to his friends
about this sage and his teachings.

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:24.400
And given that most of his friends were hippies
themselves, they were pretty open to ideas

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:25.400
of peace and enlightenment.

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:30.500
Eventually, they would get to a point where
they wanted to go meet Neem Karoli Baba in

00:07:30.500 --> 00:07:35.479
real life, but they just had one issue: they
were still broke.

00:07:35.479 --> 00:07:39.550
But, as fate would have it, an opportunity
would arise.

00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:44.700
At the time, Jobs was working as a consultant
at a game development company called Atari.

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:48.639
One of their most popular games was pong,
you’ve probably played it.

00:07:48.639 --> 00:07:53.409
Pong gave Atari international notoriety and
a company from Germany would buy a whole warehouse

00:07:53.409 --> 00:07:55.520
full of circuit boards from Atari.

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:59.860
Unfortunately though, Atari didn’t quite
make a good first impression given that half

00:07:59.860 --> 00:08:02.750
the boards didn’t end up working out of
the box.

00:08:02.750 --> 00:08:07.229
Naturally, the German company wasn’t very
happy and they wanted someone to come out

00:08:07.229 --> 00:08:08.930
there a fix them.

00:08:08.930 --> 00:08:13.860
Initially, Atari’s founder Nolan Bushnell
sent over a tech guy to Germany, but this

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:17.960
guy just made things worse, so Nolan turned
to Jobs.

00:08:17.960 --> 00:08:22.379
He told Jobs that he would book him a first
class round trip ticket to Germany and a 5

00:08:22.379 --> 00:08:25.349
star hotel if he could go fix this issue.

00:08:25.349 --> 00:08:28.750
Jobs agreed to take the job, but he had one
condition.

00:08:28.750 --> 00:08:33.190
He wanted a one way ticket to Germany and
wanted the rest of the travel expenses in

00:08:33.190 --> 00:08:34.190
cash.

00:08:34.190 --> 00:08:38.899
Additionally, he negotiated that once the
job was done, he was gone and that’s exactly

00:08:38.899 --> 00:08:40.029
what happened.

00:08:40.029 --> 00:08:43.820
He finished the job in Germany and he went
to India with Dan Kottke.

00:08:43.820 --> 00:08:48.110
Unfortunately though, India didn’t quite
live up to his expectations.

00:08:48.110 --> 00:08:51.920
First of all, the guy they were trying to
meet, Neem Karoli Baba, had already died a

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:55.660
year before this, and they simply hadn’t
heard the news yet.

00:08:55.660 --> 00:09:00.589
Jobs also quote “found India far poorer
than he had imagined and was struck by the

00:09:00.589 --> 00:09:04.330
incongruity between the country's condition
and its airs of holiness."

00:09:04.330 --> 00:09:09.130
But, by far the worst part of the trip was
the constant sickness.

00:09:09.130 --> 00:09:14.279
The duo picked up lice, infections, and worst
of all, uncontrollable diarrhea.

00:09:14.279 --> 00:09:19.839
By the end of the trip, Jobs had lost a bunch
of weight, but what he didn’t lose was his

00:09:19.839 --> 00:09:20.839
belief.

00:09:20.839 --> 00:09:26.800
While his belief in Hinduism decreased, his
belief in Buddhism became stronger than ever.

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:32.120
REFUSING SURGERY
When Jobs returned to the United States, he

00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:35.329
implemented many buddhist principles into
his day to day life.

00:09:35.329 --> 00:09:39.790
One of his strongest Buddhist beliefs was
that everything happens for a reason and that

00:09:39.790 --> 00:09:41.700
nothing is random.

00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:46.090
This was one of the ways that he coped with
being fired from Apple in 80s.

00:09:46.090 --> 00:09:49.560
He eventually came to accept that none of
the successes that followed this event like

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:54.120
Pixar and the iPhone would’ve taken place
if hadn’t been fired from Apple.

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:58.040
He also adopted a much more pragmatic perspective
when it came to death.

00:09:58.040 --> 00:10:02.740
“Remembering that I’ll be dead soon is
the most important tool I’ve ever encountered

00:10:02.740 --> 00:10:05.899
to help me make the big choices in life.

00:10:05.899 --> 00:10:10.779
Because almost everything — all external
expectations, all pride, all fear of embarrassment

00:10:10.779 --> 00:10:15.800
or failure — these things just fall away
in the face of death, leaving only what is

00:10:15.800 --> 00:10:17.100
truly important.”

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:20.959
This may have been one of the reasons that
he refused to get surgery.

00:10:20.959 --> 00:10:25.870
From his perspective, getting surgery seemed
unnatural and intrusive.

00:10:25.870 --> 00:10:29.500
And this went against all of his hippie and
buddhist beliefs.

00:10:29.500 --> 00:10:31.460
Jobs did eventually make the leap though.

00:10:31.460 --> 00:10:36.640
By the time he died, he would go from refusing
treatment to trying to get experimental treatment.

00:10:36.640 --> 00:10:41.560
In 2009 for example, Jobs would travel to
Switzerland to experiment with a radioactive

00:10:41.560 --> 00:10:42.720
isotope.

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:47.160
The isotope was supposed to attack the faulty
hormone-producing cells in the body.

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:51.170
He would also try to use his money to accelerate
the process of getting a liver which many

00:10:51.170 --> 00:10:53.430
people felt was unfair.

00:10:53.430 --> 00:10:58.700
He didn’t cut lines or do anything illegal,
but what he did do was sign up onto liver

00:10:58.700 --> 00:11:00.920
transplant lists around the world.

00:11:00.920 --> 00:11:04.110
This increased the chances that he would get
a matching donor somewhere.

00:11:04.110 --> 00:11:09.250
Personally, I don’t think this is a problem
given that it’s only natural that you try

00:11:09.250 --> 00:11:12.310
to use all the resources you have to try to
save your life.

00:11:12.310 --> 00:11:16.750
But, some like to argue that this is not something
that the average person would be able to do

00:11:16.750 --> 00:11:19.350
and so, it’s unethical.

00:11:19.350 --> 00:11:22.300
Let me know which side you guys stand on down
below.

00:11:22.300 --> 00:11:28.459
But, anyway, the saddest part of this all
is that Jobs would eventually regret his early

00:11:28.459 --> 00:11:29.459
choices.

00:11:29.459 --> 00:11:34.430
He admitted that he was wrong for rejecting
professional help, and this is just made worse

00:11:34.430 --> 00:11:38.330
by cancer experts constantly affirming that
he could still be alive if he had just had

00:11:38.330 --> 00:11:39.750
surgery earlier.

00:11:39.750 --> 00:11:47.269
But, in the end, everyone dies at somepoint,
but not everyone lives.

00:11:47.269 --> 00:11:50.560
This, however, was never the case with Steve
Jobs.

00:11:50.560 --> 00:11:55.050
Since the age of 17, he lived by the quote,
“If you live each day as if it was your

00:11:55.050 --> 00:11:58.339
last, someday you’ll most certainly be right.”

00:11:58.339 --> 00:12:03.310
And considering his impact on the world, there’s
no doubt that he lived by this slogan till

00:12:03.310 --> 00:12:04.740
the very last day.

00:12:04.740 --> 00:12:07.020
Are you a fan of Steve Jobs?

00:12:07.020 --> 00:12:08.570
